Falsifies `paging::x86_64::index_is_always_in_bounds`.

The defect: the table is declared with 256 entries. This is the half of the property the other
two records do not touch: the harness does not assert `index < 512`, it asserts `index <
ENTRIES`, and `ENTRIES` is what sizes `PageTable::entries`. So the proof is a statement that
the mask and the array agree, and it is worth having precisely because the two are written in
different files with no shared constant between them: `index` masks a literal `0x1ff`, and
`PageTable` is `[u64; ENTRIES]`. Shrink one and the walk writes off the end of a table with
nothing else in the crate objecting.

Expected to fail: `assert!(Ia32e::index(va, level) < crate::ENTRIES)`, for any `va` whose
selected field is 256 or more. A wrong `ENTRIES` also gives `PageTable` the wrong size, but
nothing in the crate asserts that size, which is the point.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** DECISIONS §19 (architectural parity) has each ISA state the
property itself; the `aarch64` and `sv39` records break the mask in two other ways.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -236,7 +236,7 @@ impl PageSize {
 }
 
